          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Sheol is the grave, death, or the shadowy realm/state of the dead—the common destination of righteous and wicked—and is distinct from Gehenna or final punitive hell.\",\"book_summary\":\"Sheol is mortality’s claim upon all flesh, personified as a devouring place holding righteous and wicked dead in silence until they are released at resurrection; Sheol is then destroyed and is distinct from Gehenna.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The common realm or state of the dead, distinct from final judgment.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The common place or power of death that claims all flesh, distinct from final judgment.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material disagreement over who or what falls under Sheol: both cover the dead generally, righteous and wicked alike. The book additionally adopts the disputed unconscious-sleep account of the dead’s condition, but this does not change the minimum core identification or covered cases.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book’s resurrection and destruction language is compatible with the consensus description of Sheol/Hades as temporary and distinct from Gehenna.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum cores, both readings identify Sheol with death/the grave or realm of the dead rather than final punitive hell. The book’s personification, sleep language, resurrection sequence, and proposed derivation from sha'al add theological and lexical detail without replacing the central referent or changing its extension.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed passages strongly support Sheol as the grave, its personification as devouring, the dead’s silence, its eventual surrender of the dead, and its destruction. They do not by themselves establish the proposed etymology from sha'al ('to demand'), and the claim of literal unconscious sleep goes beyond what all the cited texts unambiguously require.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
